"""Class 10 SST Ch 14 Federalism - extras."""
from common import AR4, Q

AR = [
 AR4(r"India is a federation, yet the Union government is more powerful than the states.", r"The Union List has subjects like defence, foreign affairs and currency, and residuary subjects go to the Union government.", "a",
     r"Important national subjects and the residuary powers lie with the Union, which explains its greater strength."),
 AR4(r"Education and forests are subjects in the Concurrent List.", r"Both the Union and the state governments can make laws on Concurrent List subjects.", "b",
     r"Both are true, but R describes the list rather than explaining why education and forests are in it."),
 AR4(r"In India, the power of making laws on police and agriculture lies with the state governments.", r"Police and agriculture are subjects in the Concurrent List.", "c",
     r"A is true as both are State List subjects. R is false: they are not in the Concurrent List."),
 AR4(r"The Constitution of India declares Hindi as the national language of the country.", r"The Constitution gives Hindi the status of the official language, and 22 languages in all are listed in the Eighth Schedule.", "d",
     r"A is false: no language has been declared the national language. R is true as far as Hindi being the official language; 22 languages are in the Eighth Schedule."),
 AR4(r"India's federalism is of the 'holding together' type.", r"In such a federation a large country divides its power between the constituent states and the national government.", "a",
     r"India's federation was formed by dividing power among states and the Centre in a large country; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Some Indian states, such as Nagaland, Uttarakhand and Jharkhand, were created on grounds other than language.", r"Local governments in India were given constitutional status in 1992.", "b",
     r"A is true (culture, ethnicity or geography). R is also true but unrelated, so it does not explain A."),
]

CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> The Constitution of India divides powers between the Union and the states in three lists. Defence, foreign affairs, banking and currency are in one list. Police, trade, commerce, agriculture and irrigation are in the second. Education, forest, trade unions, marriage and adoption are in the third. A subject that does not fall in any list is dealt with in a special way. "
   r"(i) In which list is defence? (ii) Which list has police and agriculture? (iii) Who can make laws on a concurrent subject? (iv) Who has the power on subjects not in any list? Why is this significant?",
   r"(i) Union List (1). (ii) State List (1). (iii) Both the Union and state governments (1). (iv) The Union government (residuary subjects); it adds to the strength of the Centre, because new subjects like computer software come under the Union (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In a district town, the municipal corporation is headed by an elected Mayor. In a nearby village, the gram sabha elects the sarpanch, and the panchayat samiti works at the block level. A Zilla Parishad works at the district level. A student says that in 1992 these bodies got constitutional status and regular elections. "
   r"(i) Who heads a municipal corporation? (ii) Name the body that includes all voters of a village. (iii) Which level is above the block-level samiti? (iv) Why is it important that local governments have regular elections?",
   r"(i) The Mayor (1). (ii) Gram Sabha (1). (iii) Zilla Parishad (1). (iv) Regular elections made by the State Election Commissioner give people a say in local decisions, make local government accountable and keep it from being dissolved arbitrarily by the state government (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> A candidate wants to appear for a Central Government examination. She learns that the Constitution did not give the status of national language to any one language. Hindi was identified as the official language, but it is the mother tongue of only about 40 per cent of Indians. Besides Hindi, 21 other languages are recognised as Scheduled Languages. "
   r"(i) Which language was identified as the official language? (ii) How many languages are included in the Eighth Schedule? (iii) Does India have a national language? (iv) Why did the Constitution include safeguards for other languages?",
   r"(i) Hindi (1). (ii) 22 (1). (iii) No (1). (iv) Hindi is the mother tongue of only about 40 per cent of the people, so safeguards protect other languages and avoid imposition, which helps keep a diverse country united (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

MISTAKES = [
 r"Mixing up the lists: <b>defence, foreign affairs, banking, currency</b> = Union; <b>police, trade, agriculture, irrigation</b> = State; <b>education, forests, marriage, adoption</b> = Concurrent. <b>Residuary subjects</b> go to the Union.",
 r"Writing that Hindi is the <b>national language</b>: India has <b>no</b> national language; Hindi is the official language and 22 languages are in the Eighth Schedule.",
 r"Mixing up 'coming together' (USA, Switzerland, Australia: independent states unite) with 'holding together' (India, Spain, Belgium: a large country shares power).",
 r"Saying all states were created on the basis of language: <b>Nagaland, Uttarakhand and Jharkhand</b> were created to recognise culture, ethnicity or geography; local governments got constitutional status in <b>1992</b>.",
 r"Saying a municipal corporation is headed by a chairperson: it is headed by the <b>Mayor</b>; a municipality is headed by a <b>chairperson</b>.",
 r"Naming 'gram sabha' as the executive body: it is the <b>general body of all voters</b> in the village; the panchayat is its executive.",
]
